│                               Ring of Nestor                                │
└─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘

The Ring of Nestor is the name associated with a gold ring discussed in Minoan 
and Mycenaean scholarship and with Arthur J. Evans’s 1925 publication The Ring 
of Nestor: A Glimpse into the Minoan After-World and a Sepulchral Treasure of  
Gold Signet-Rings and Bead-Seals from Thisbe, Boeotia.[4][5] Separate secondary
descriptions identify a golden ring from a tomb at Isopata, near Knossos, dated
broadly to 1600–1400 BC, but the supplied records do not securely demonstrate  
that this Isopata object is identical with the artifact named in Evans’s       
title.[7][8][13]                                                               

The ring’s imagery has been interpreted in relation to a Minoan after-world. A 
2011 peer-reviewed article by Nanno Marinatos and Briana Jackson treats the    
so-called Nestor Ring as an object of iconographic and authenticity analysis,  
arguing that its semantic coherence depends on Egyptian prototypes while its   
butterfly motif weighs against authenticity.[1] The same study reports that Sir
Arthur Evans accepted the ring as authentic, that other scholars condemned it  
as a forgery, and that later scholars renewed claims for authenticity; its     
authors also judge the reported find circumstances suspicious.[1]              

Because the available evidence gives the named publication a Thisbê, Boeotia   
context but gives the separately described Isopata ring a Knossos context, the 
object’s precise provenance and identity remain unresolved.[4][5][7][8][13] The
authenticity question should therefore be presented as a dispute in the        
scholarship rather than as a settled classification.[1]                        


                     Discovery, provenance and publication                     


