engraved scene as a glimpse of the Minoan Underworld; a 1936 text records his  
description of microscopic engraving with seventeen figures.[11] Theodore      
Eliopoulos’s 2013 article is another identifiable scholarly contribution to the
ring’s iconography, but only its bibliographic record and an AI-generated      
summary are present in the supplied material, so its specific conclusions      
cannot be used to resolve authenticity.[3]                                     

The provenance evidence remains a separate problem. Secondary sources describe 
